WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:02.860
Film Courage: Bing, was there a day that filmmaking
mattered to you more than skateboarding?

00:00:02.860 --> 00:00:06.990
Bing Liu, filmmaker of MINDING THE GAP: There
were some days that it did and some days that

00:00:06.990 --> 00:00:10.700
it didn’t.

00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:17.260
So when I was 17 I watched the film SLACKER
and I took the freaks and geeks of my community

00:00:17.260 --> 00:00:23.540
in Rockford [Illinois] and I sort of had them
improv them talking about their lives, bumping

00:00:23.540 --> 00:00:25.839
into each other, it was very Slackeresque.

00:00:25.839 --> 00:00:32.640
On that day filmmaking mattered more to me
than skateboarding and then I kept making

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:40.440
skate videos and my next film that I did I
cared more about filmmaking, I don’t know

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:41.440
if I’m answering my question or not?

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:42.440
Film Courage: You are.

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:45.530
I mean whenever you take on a project whether
it’s having a Youtube channel or doing a

00:00:45.530 --> 00:00:49.550
documentary, it takes over your life in so
many ways, like you basically have no free

00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:54.240
time, like this is pretty much what you’re
doing for a good chunk of time.

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:57.570
Was there a point where you said “If I’m
going to put skateboarding on the back burner

00:00:57.570 --> 00:01:07.560
and to me making this film is everything and
I need to do it for me.”

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:10.790
Bing: I mean skateboarding…I feel like it
was never that choice, there was never that

00:01:10.790 --> 00:01:11.890
fork in the road.

00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:14.700
I was 24 when I started doing this film.

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:19.200
I really wanted to explore these themes of
family and violence and childhood so I went

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:22.770
around the country and interviewed skateboarders
from all over the place.

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:26.509
It confirmed my belief that there are a lot
of patterns in the community.

00:01:26.509 --> 00:01:28.979
And then a year in I met Keire [one of the
three stories in MINDING THE GAP] and I was

00:01:28.979 --> 00:01:34.810
like “Oh, this person hasn’t processed
it in the ways that other people have.”

00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:36.610
Later in the documentary Zack [one of the
three stories in MINDING THE GAP] is like

00:01:36.610 --> 00:01:39.360
“Yeah of course my father beat me.”

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:42.900
I feel like most people brushed it off like
that.

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:47.450
There is something that had hardened over
the softness that Keire still held onto.

00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:50.810
And that’s why I saw myself in Keire.

00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:58.719
Keire is willing to do the work of processing
it, to not just brush it off, to make it something

00:01:58.719 --> 00:02:01.539
that mattered to him and that he wanted to
change.

00:02:01.539 --> 00:02:06.119
Film Courage: Absolutely, he was incredibly
open and I really appreciate that.

00:02:06.119 --> 00:02:10.680
Speaking of which (and Zack), I think there
was one scene early on in the film where Zack

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:13.840
kind of looked at you (he might have been
smoking or doing something) and he said “Are

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:14.840
you going to film this?”

00:02:14.840 --> 00:02:18.690
And then he says “You know what, you can
pretty much film whatever you want.”

00:02:18.690 --> 00:02:23.601
How do you earn that level of trust with someone,
even a friend because this thing [gestures

00:02:23.601 --> 00:02:26.100
to the camera] can shape someone in any way
they want it to in the editing room?

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:29.170
How do you earn that level of trust?

00:02:29.170 --> 00:02:33.820
Bing: I think it was just a five year long
conversation about representation.

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:40.230
That was one of many moments both on and off
camera where we talked about what was going

00:02:40.230 --> 00:02:44.150
to go in the film, how they felt about what
we had captured so far.

00:02:44.150 --> 00:02:49.030
And sometimes they were light and funny like
that smoking moment.

00:02:49.030 --> 00:02:53.960
But other times it was like “Zack how do
you feel about me having film you run off

00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:55.130
to Denver?

00:02:55.130 --> 00:02:59.750
How do you feel about you sort of abandoning
your son?”

00:02:59.750 --> 00:03:05.680
You know so they understood that I was checking
in, that I cared.

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:09.500
And I told them a couple of years before we
finished the film that they were going to

00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:13.190
get a chance to see the film before it was
picture locked and weigh in.

00:03:13.190 --> 00:03:19.400
Film Courage: I’m wondering when you actually
started filming in general whether they were

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:21.800
skateboarding videos or whatever?

00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:25.340
When was the first time you picked up a camera?

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:27.590
Bing: 14 maybe?

00:03:27.590 --> 00:03:29.360
Film Courage: So pre-DSLR I guess?

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:30.360
Bing: Pre-DSLR.

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:38.200
I mean mini-DV tape was the height of technology
at the time and then soon after recording

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:40.540
to DVD came and then recording to hard drive
cameras.

00:03:40.540 --> 00:03:44.970
Film Courage: I believe initially you were
not intending to put yourself in the film.

00:03:44.970 --> 00:03:48.930
And I can see why, I know a lot of people
have their own take on that.

00:03:48.930 --> 00:03:51.870
It definitely works, it absolutely works that
you’re in the film.

00:03:51.870 --> 00:03:56.880
I think you said that at some point your editor
and you sat down and you edited all three

00:03:56.880 --> 00:03:58.820
stories separately to see if they worked?

00:03:58.820 --> 00:04:01.070
Can you talk about how that worked?

00:04:01.070 --> 00:04:09.480
Bing: I met Josh [Altman] last year editing
a different project and I convinced him to

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:14.120
come on to consult at first because he didn’t
have time to edit fully and he told me you

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:21.840
should try editing every story on its own
to get it working and so I did that and on

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:26.470
the first try with Keire and Zack stories
it was like “Okay, we have a film here for

00:04:26.470 --> 00:04:28.030
sure that can get into Sundance.”

00:04:28.030 --> 00:04:29.280
And I was like “What?”

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:33.830
Because that was the first time someone had
told me that.

00:04:33.830 --> 00:04:38.650
And then for my story it was like “We’re
not sure how this is going to work?

00:04:38.650 --> 00:04:40.630
We’ll figure it out later.

00:04:40.630 --> 00:04:44.930
So we wove Zack and Keire’s stories together.

00:04:44.930 --> 00:04:53.880
You know ultimately it was finding, it was
sort of characterizing the camera as the character

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:56.730
to ultimately lead up to the mom interview.

00:04:56.730 --> 00:05:02.220
Film Courage: How was that to know that the
film could go to Sundance and beyond?

00:05:02.220 --> 00:05:05.480
I mean most people hope for that and they
don’t actually get it.

00:05:05.480 --> 00:05:10.360
But then you’re filming this incredibly
personal story (for all parties involved)

00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:12.530
to know that “Oh my gosh, this is happening?”

00:05:12.530 --> 00:05:14.920
Bing: Because I wasn’t doing it for Sundance.

00:05:14.920 --> 00:05:18.780
I mean there was a large part of me that wasn’t
doing it just to make a film.

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:22.940
It was so important to me that I would have
just wanted to do it.

00:05:22.940 --> 00:05:27.950
The goal is to get it seen very widely so
it can make a difference, I mean Sundance

00:05:27.950 --> 00:05:29.980
was great for that purpose.

00:05:29.980 --> 00:05:35.840
But I’m very much a realist and I’m just
like and I very much live in the moment.

00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:38.860
I mean I’m still just sort of waiting to
wake up and “Oh?

00:05:38.860 --> 00:05:40.200
None of that happened.”

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:43.170
Of course, the last nine months was just a
dream.

00:05:43.170 --> 00:05:47.620
Film Courage: I think you had said in a Q&amp;A
or maybe it was someone else that what is

00:05:47.620 --> 00:05:52.070
effective about MINDING THE GAP is also what
is not being said.

00:05:52.070 --> 00:05:55.980
Is there anything you want to talk about that
in terms of some of the things you just show

00:05:55.980 --> 00:06:00.510
and some of the things you leave out and the
audience can kind of fill in the gaps (and

00:06:00.510 --> 00:06:06.860
no pun intended) in terms of what is happening
or some of the emptiness around some of the

00:06:06.860 --> 00:06:08.280
lives of the skateboarders?

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:14.080
Bing: I think that first year those interviews
were really on the nose.

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:21.160
And that first assembly cut was very much
just people really directly talking about

00:06:21.160 --> 00:06:25.010
abuse and violence.

00:06:25.010 --> 00:06:32.330
It was just you know really hard to watch
and for many reasons.

00:06:32.330 --> 00:06:39.750
And once it became more about a story of Zack
and Keire growing up and these issues of abuse

00:06:39.750 --> 00:06:46.470
and trauma coming out organically as obstacles
to their growing up I think it became a lot

00:06:46.470 --> 00:06:50.860
more emotionally grappling.

00:06:50.860 --> 00:06:55.660
A little does a lot and I think ultimately
that was how we were able to fit so many themes

00:06:55.660 --> 00:07:02.520
that are in the film, race, class, gender
relations, masculinity.

00:07:02.520 --> 00:07:08.280
If you really just look at the mechanics of
this story, there is really just a one-two-punch

00:07:08.280 --> 00:07:09.750
for some of these themes.

00:07:09.750 --> 00:07:16.570
Like for race (for example) there are really
three moments that deal with race in the film

00:07:16.570 --> 00:07:21.030
but it says everything you need to know about
how this affects Keire.

00:07:21.030 --> 00:07:28.580
Film Courage: Can you talk about the types
of filming where you want the subjects to

00:07:28.580 --> 00:07:34.510
pretend that the camera was not there and
then filming where you do want the subjects

00:07:34.510 --> 00:07:36.150
to look straight at the camera?

00:07:36.150 --> 00:07:39.880
How did you balance that or was this not even
conscious?

00:07:39.880 --> 00:07:41.240
It was just you were filming?

00:07:41.240 --> 00:07:47.720
Bing: I wasn’t really conscious, I mean
I certainly adhered to vérité (the school

00:07:47.720 --> 00:07:55.400
of vérité) which is that you just hang out
and something happens and you start filming.

00:07:55.400 --> 00:08:03.980
For me I feel like I watched a lot of vérité
films that showed how the camera effected

00:08:03.980 --> 00:08:09.000
the story and the presence of the camera and
there is something actually more truthful

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:10.000
to that.

00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:15.520
So I think that’s why we left in moments
like Zack asking me like “Do you want me

00:08:15.520 --> 00:08:18.530
to pretend like the camera is not there or
what?”

00:08:18.530 --> 00:08:25.190
Because it just sort of nods to the fact that
yeah we’re both constructing it and we’re

00:08:25.190 --> 00:08:29.780
filming things and the camera might affect
how they’re acting but you can’t always

00:08:29.780 --> 00:08:34.440
do that (it would be exhausting), so there’s
more truth to that.

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:37.950
Film Courage: We asked some of our viewers
questions ahead of time.

00:08:37.950 --> 00:08:43.060
We just let them know that it was a documentary
but we didn’t tell them too much but they

00:08:43.060 --> 00:08:49.010
wanted to know, the first viewer asked (his
name is ZombieDude64)…

00:08:49.010 --> 00:08:52.740
Bing: I love this, this is awesome.

00:08:52.740 --> 00:08:57.510
Film Courage: And I’m assuming it’s a
he, I haven’t looked at his account “So

00:08:57.510 --> 00:09:01.279
what are your tips on finding screenings for
the film?”

00:09:01.279 --> 00:09:02.410
Bing: Finding screenings?

00:09:02.410 --> 00:09:03.790
Like feedback screenings?

00:09:03.790 --> 00:09:08.850
Film Courage: Yeah, how did you arrange screenings
or was it all festival submissions and that’s

00:09:08.850 --> 00:09:09.850
how?

00:09:09.850 --> 00:09:10.850
Bing: Oh, I see.

00:09:10.850 --> 00:09:17.200
Well I mean getting into Sundance positions
you really well for festivals to come to you.

00:09:17.200 --> 00:09:22.800
A friend of mine Stephen Elliott he’s a
filmmaker who did this journalistic piece

00:09:22.800 --> 00:09:28.740
about film fest fees - you should Google it,
I mean it’s really telling about sort of

00:09:28.740 --> 00:09:33.250
the behind-the-curtains look of how some of
the films get accepted into festivals and

00:09:33.250 --> 00:09:35.880
the whole micro-economy behind that.

00:09:35.880 --> 00:09:45.810
And there is unfairness there but I think
it speaks to being a filmmaker who is independent

00:09:45.810 --> 00:09:50.700
making their first film and having two learn
how to play the game while also learning the

00:09:50.700 --> 00:09:54.050
rules of the game at the same time and it
can be really frustrating.

00:09:54.050 --> 00:09:57.290
But I think these things always change.

00:09:57.290 --> 00:10:02.430
I don’t know I mean maybe the next film
I make will be distributed in a different

00:10:02.430 --> 00:10:06.740
way, I’ll have to apply more but for the
most part we got into Sundance, won an award

00:10:06.740 --> 00:10:12.960
and basically every fest we got into has asked
for our film.

00:10:12.960 --> 00:10:17.270
And it’s not like automatic either, a lot
of festivals still reject us.

00:10:17.270 --> 00:10:20.530
It’s just that they want to come to us and
ask us for a screener.

00:10:20.530 --> 00:10:25.330
Film Courage: At what point did the film become
a movie about fathers?

00:10:25.330 --> 00:10:27.029
I think you said you set out to just film
people about family?

00:10:27.029 --> 00:10:29.910
Bing: It was set out to be like that.

00:10:29.910 --> 00:10:32.060
I asked every skateboarder about that.

00:10:32.060 --> 00:10:35.800
It was sort of built in thematically at the
beginning.

00:10:35.800 --> 00:10:39.770
All the themes are built in thematically it’s
just the way that the themes were told shifted

00:10:39.770 --> 00:10:40.770
over time.

00:10:40.770 --> 00:10:44.390
Film Courage: I don’t know if I’ll keep
this part in.

00:10:44.390 --> 00:10:48.000
I know the interviewer is not supposed to
reveal too much but having grown up without

00:10:48.000 --> 00:10:52.600
a father (I had like one phone conversation
with him, that was it), I noticed looking

00:10:52.600 --> 00:10:57.690
back on my life that every friend I had, they
had a missing parent and then I had this connection

00:10:57.690 --> 00:11:00.810
with them and I didn’t even realize that
consciously [until later].

00:11:00.810 --> 00:11:05.899
Is that something may you saw with the film
that a lot of the people who bonded were able

00:11:05.899 --> 00:11:11.020
to bond in a way that was…there was stuff
that was not said but maybe because they’d

00:11:11.020 --> 00:11:13.170
had a missing so to speak?

00:11:13.170 --> 00:11:19.520
Bing: Yeah, I think heartache really finds
affinity with heartache, I certainly felt

00:11:19.520 --> 00:11:20.660
that way.

00:11:20.660 --> 00:11:26.930
I was friends with people who knew some deep
level of pain and I enjoyed being around them

00:11:26.930 --> 00:11:30.420
because it made me feel less alone in my situation.

00:11:30.420 --> 00:11:35.610
Otherwise when I think I was hanging around
people who were more white bread or vanilla

00:11:35.610 --> 00:11:40.500
with their situations at home, it did make
me feel a little less than and made me feel

00:11:40.500 --> 00:11:43.040
bad about myself even though it wasn’t my
own fault.

00:11:43.040 --> 00:11:45.120
You know, when you’re a kid you can’t
help that.

00:11:45.120 --> 00:11:53.959
So hanging out with people that shared my
pain ultimately it was like an ad-hoc family

00:11:53.959 --> 00:11:54.959
all of its own.

00:11:54.959 --> 00:11:58.640
Film Courage: I used to dread going to friends’
houses that would have the big family dinners

00:11:58.640 --> 00:12:03.690
because I wasn’t used to that and it was
so foreign to me and to know like “Okay,

00:12:03.690 --> 00:12:06.560
well you have to wait for so and so to speak
first.”

00:12:06.560 --> 00:12:08.350
These unwritten rules.

00:12:08.350 --> 00:12:14.899
Bing: It’s super weird and what it does
though it sort of enlightens you to the constructedness

00:12:14.899 --> 00:12:20.380
of these things and ultimately to everything
and maybe this is a system of this because

00:12:20.380 --> 00:12:25.160
I went to school for English and it was more
like theory and criticism but my big takeaway

00:12:25.160 --> 00:12:31.399
is like everything is constructed and we can
deconstruct everything and so I don’t know

00:12:31.399 --> 00:12:37.470
I feel like I was primed for that in the situations
growing up where I just felt like I was coat

00:12:37.470 --> 00:12:42.190
switching, I felt like an outsider in that
I was performing to fit in.

00:12:42.190 --> 00:12:46.110
Film Courage: How many years were you making
the film?

00:12:46.110 --> 00:12:49.360
When did you begin and when was the final
edit?

00:12:49.360 --> 00:12:53.670
Bing: I was about 24 when I began filming.

00:12:53.670 --> 00:12:56.240
I was editing all along the way in my bedroom.

00:12:56.240 --> 00:13:02.399
We got funding late 2016 and then we started
our search for an editor.

00:13:02.399 --> 00:13:04.660
By that point I had a pretty decent rough
cut.

00:13:04.660 --> 00:13:08.390
I was like if we’re going to be paying someone
$2,500.00 a week, they better be good.

00:13:08.390 --> 00:13:12.709
But the problem is in documentary at least
the really good editors are booked like a

00:13:12.709 --> 00:13:14.250
year or two in advance.

00:13:14.250 --> 00:13:18.110
I was really lucky I met Josh working on another
project.

00:13:18.110 --> 00:13:21.930
He looked at the rough cut and he agreed to
come on to consult and then he had a few months

00:13:21.930 --> 00:13:24.330
free to jump in with me.

00:13:24.330 --> 00:13:30.950
So that was sort of the big picture process
of crafting the film.

00:13:30.950 --> 00:13:36.160
It wasn’t until the last few months that
I dug into archival footage and built up the

00:13:36.160 --> 00:13:42.029
sense of this is just a skate video that turned
into something else.

00:13:42.029 --> 00:13:44.550
And even with Keire I didn’t know him growing
up.

00:13:44.550 --> 00:13:46.360
I accidentally filmed a clip of him fighting.

00:13:46.360 --> 00:13:49.620
I had to source of a lot of his footage from
other people that were more his age because

00:13:49.620 --> 00:13:52.230
he’s 7 or 8 years younger than me.

00:13:52.230 --> 00:13:55.490
Zack I had more footage of because we actually
were closer in age.

00:13:55.490 --> 00:13:57.709
Film Courage: We had another viewer write
in.

00:13:57.709 --> 00:13:58.709
Bing: What’s the viewer’s name?

00:13:58.709 --> 00:14:03.080
Film Courage: His name is John Dimelu (and
I’m sorry John if I’m saying your name

00:14:03.080 --> 00:14:04.080
incorrectly).

00:14:04.080 --> 00:14:06.050
So he asks “How do you know when you really
have a story to tell?

00:14:06.050 --> 00:14:08.019
And why do you choose to tell it?”

00:14:08.019 --> 00:14:11.200
So at what point did you know, okay this is
taking shape?

00:14:11.200 --> 00:14:19.160
This is more than just interviews about family
and why did you go ahead to take the final

00:14:19.160 --> 00:14:24.580
leap and find an editor that costs $2,500
a week?

00:14:24.580 --> 00:14:31.860
Bing: I think I was ready to take the plunge
when I did the fellowship with Kartemquin

00:14:31.860 --> 00:14:34.580
[Kartemquin Films, based in Chicago, Illinois]
and watched a lot of vérité films and just

00:14:34.580 --> 00:14:39.490
trusted the process of these characters lives
are going to lead…I mean it’s all about

00:14:39.490 --> 00:14:40.490
casting, too.

00:14:40.490 --> 00:14:47.050
I saw in Keire this opportunity to capture
his internal transformation and processing

00:14:47.050 --> 00:14:50.360
both with his father and race and growing
up.

00:14:50.360 --> 00:14:54.920
For Zack it was pretty clear that he was going
to have some issues with becoming a young

00:14:54.920 --> 00:14:55.920
father.

00:14:55.920 --> 00:14:57.530
Like he wasn’t quite ready for it.

00:14:57.530 --> 00:15:01.420
Our second shoot we did together with Zack
I went into the hospital.

00:15:01.420 --> 00:15:05.830
Nina [another young woman featured in the
film] was getting induced for labor because

00:15:05.830 --> 00:15:07.630
she was late.

00:15:07.630 --> 00:15:12.570
They botched her epidurals, she flatlined
it was very traumatic for her.

00:15:12.570 --> 00:15:14.050
We did a long interview with her afterwards.

00:15:14.050 --> 00:15:16.651
I mean that was the beginning of it.

00:15:16.651 --> 00:15:22.450
It was like "Okay, this is going to be a film.”

00:15:22.450 --> 00:15:27.450
Film Courage: Did you tell your subjects (and
I know they were really more like friends)

00:15:27.450 --> 00:15:30.310
that whatever you filmed would end up in the
movie?

00:15:30.310 --> 00:15:34.180
Or did you allow them…I know you said at
the beginning [of our interview] that everybody

00:15:34.180 --> 00:15:37.339
got to see it before it was finally released.

00:15:37.339 --> 00:15:41.830
But was that something along the way where
you said “If you don’t like it, I’ll

00:15:41.830 --> 00:15:42.830
take it out.”

00:15:42.830 --> 00:15:46.090
Or “everything I film is going to end up
here?”

00:15:46.090 --> 00:15:53.660
Bing: It was a five year conversation about
what was going to be in the film or not.

00:15:53.660 --> 00:16:00.950
And there was never once where they were like
“Don’t put this in the film.”

00:16:00.950 --> 00:16:05.399
Except for when we were dealing with Nina
and what she wanted in the film in terms of

00:16:05.399 --> 00:16:08.600
her relationship with Zack and the abuse.

00:16:08.600 --> 00:16:14.459
And that was out of a desire to make the relationship
work.

00:16:14.459 --> 00:16:17.890
And in my head I was like “Well, she could
change her mind.”

00:16:17.890 --> 00:16:23.990
This relationship was really fickle and ultimately
if she sees the final cut and doesn’t want

00:16:23.990 --> 00:16:30.620
this aspect of the film in, we’ve got to
respect her wishes.

00:16:30.620 --> 00:16:35.590
I mean there is a famous story about Kirsten
Johnson who did CAMERAPERSON.

00:16:35.590 --> 00:16:40.370
She worked on a film for two years following
these Afghani women and at the end of the

00:16:40.370 --> 00:16:43.052
process she showed the the film and one of
them didn’t want to be in the film at all.

00:16:43.052 --> 00:16:51.870
And so it was like two or three years of work…not
down the drain but that she ethically had

00:16:51.870 --> 00:16:57.149
to put aside and that ultimately made her
think about why…”What am I doing documenting

00:16:57.149 --> 00:16:58.149
these lives?”

00:16:58.149 --> 00:16:59.310
And that led to CAMERAPERSON.

00:16:59.310 --> 00:17:06.030
Film Courage: Yeah, it’s a really fine line
because you have to at some point, put your

00:17:06.030 --> 00:17:09.009
foot down and say “This is my project.”

00:17:09.009 --> 00:17:12.639
But then it’s also someone’s life and
someone’s legacy.

00:17:12.639 --> 00:17:17.089
People still have to go about their lives
and people will recognize them from the film.

00:17:17.089 --> 00:17:20.739
Bing: I mean there’s a power dynamic at
work that is so different from journalism.

00:17:20.739 --> 00:17:24.819
You know, journalism you go in, you get some
interview bites and then you leave.

00:17:24.819 --> 00:17:32.190
We’re asking for years worth of vulnerability
and representation from these people and it

00:17:32.190 --> 00:17:35.220
would be one thing if this film were about
Anthony.

00:17:35.220 --> 00:17:39.230
He has a lot more power than Nina does.

00:17:39.230 --> 00:17:41.909
Nina especially in this group is the most
vulnerable.

00:17:41.909 --> 00:17:47.019
She doesn’t have the communal and friend
support that Keire and Zack have and she’s

00:17:47.019 --> 00:17:48.259
a woman.

00:17:48.259 --> 00:17:50.129
Everything is sort of stacked against her
in this situation.

00:17:50.129 --> 00:17:57.169
So especially her I had to listen to and I
lost sleep thinking “What if she doesn’t

00:17:57.169 --> 00:17:59.850
want this to happen?”

00:17:59.850 --> 00:18:03.409
But ultimately everyone watched the film and
they were like “You got it.

00:18:03.409 --> 00:18:06.110
You captured it.”

00:18:06.110 --> 00:18:08.889
Film Courage: Now the film [MINDING THE GAP]
is going to be released August 17th?

00:18:08.889 --> 00:18:11.279
Bing: It releases August 17th on Hulu.

00:18:11.279 --> 00:18:16.169
It will be in theaters, several across the
country and then a broadcast on POV [Point

00:18:16.169 --> 00:18:18.840
Of View on PBS] in 2019.

00:18:18.840 --> 00:18:21.950
Film Courage: How does this feel?

00:18:21.950 --> 00:18:26.641
That’s going to change things…a lot in
so many ways in terms of the reach, people

00:18:26.641 --> 00:18:27.830
knowing everybody’s story.

00:18:27.830 --> 00:18:29.510
How does that feel?

00:18:29.510 --> 00:18:31.190
Bing: I don’t know.

00:18:31.190 --> 00:18:33.570
I think I’m like disassociating right now.

00:18:33.570 --> 00:18:37.629
I mean that’s a coping mechanism I learned
when I was growing up.

00:18:37.629 --> 00:18:46.149
But I think the way I handle stress is I recognize
what I can and can’t control and I’ve

00:18:46.149 --> 00:18:50.340
literally no control over what the film is
going to do.

00:18:50.340 --> 00:18:52.639
I’m eager to see what it’s going to do.

00:18:52.639 --> 00:18:54.980
I’m not sure I feel much yet.

00:18:54.980 --> 00:19:01.889
I think probably 11:59 on August 16th I’m
going to feel nervousness but right now I’m

00:19:01.889 --> 00:19:04.309
just like “What’s for dinner?”

00:19:04.309 --> 00:19:06.570
Film Courage: You can Tweet about it.

00:19:06.570 --> 00:19:08.369
That’s the new coping mechanism.

00:19:08.369 --> 00:19:09.369
Bing: Exactly.

00:19:09.369 --> 00:19:15.100
Film Courage: Well I’m sure that people
who have been in a similar situation are going

00:19:15.100 --> 00:19:16.720
to definitely resonate with the film.

00:19:16.720 --> 00:19:25.580
And maybe it will bring up some old stuff
but I think…just…seeing it I was still

00:19:25.580 --> 00:19:30.020
thinking about it the next morning and I’m
watching people who are a total different

00:19:30.020 --> 00:19:34.211
generation than myself but seeing them go
through the exact same things that I went

00:19:34.211 --> 00:19:35.211
through.

00:19:35.211 --> 00:19:37.630
So I think that for a lot of people it may
be healing in some way.

00:19:37.630 --> 00:19:41.639
It may bring up some old stuff but I think
it’s really important for people to watch

00:19:41.639 --> 00:19:42.639
this movie.

00:19:42.639 --> 00:19:49.350
Bing: Something that…when we showed Nina
the fine cut she relived the relationship

00:19:49.350 --> 00:19:50.350
with Zack.

00:19:50.350 --> 00:19:54.820
They’d been split up for over a year at
that point but she fell back in love with

00:19:54.820 --> 00:19:57.880
him, his charisma and she was also hurt by
him again.

00:19:57.880 --> 00:20:03.539
We had to process that with her afterwards
and her feelings.

00:20:03.539 --> 00:20:08.729
There was one screening that was at a college
in the spring that she came out to and there

00:20:08.729 --> 00:20:14.820
was a domestic violence shelter there with
a booth that also drew out some people from

00:20:14.820 --> 00:20:23.320
their community and there were these women
who were twice her age who were saying “Your

00:20:23.320 --> 00:20:26.859
relationship with Zack reminds me of my relationship
with my husband.

00:20:26.859 --> 00:20:30.220
You were so brave for getting out now.

00:20:30.220 --> 00:20:31.909
You’re so brave for telling your story.”

00:20:31.909 --> 00:20:35.570
I could just see it where all of these women
were just pouring their hearts out to her

00:20:35.570 --> 00:20:39.960
that Nina was realizing that her story was
bigger than her own.

00:20:39.960 --> 00:20:45.950
I mean I have dozens of stories like that
where there’s just been these really personal

00:20:45.950 --> 00:20:53.279
effects in people that is going to translate
I think to them being a different father,

00:20:53.279 --> 00:20:55.619
them being a different person emotionally.

00:20:55.619 --> 00:21:00.480
It’s been pretty amazing so far and I hope
it continues to have that impact.

00:21:00.480 --> 00:21:05.080
Film Courage: We had another question that
came in, it’s much more light-hearted.

00:21:05.080 --> 00:21:06.820
Bing: What’s the user name?

00:21:06.820 --> 00:21:08.899
Film Courage: His name is Nafis Mustafa.

00:21:08.899 --> 00:21:09.899
Bing: Okay.

00:21:09.899 --> 00:21:13.019
I’m just hoping for another ZombieDude64
or something?

00:21:13.019 --> 00:21:15.669
Film Courage: And Nafis, he has commented
before.

00:21:15.669 --> 00:21:16.669
He’s been great.

00:21:16.669 --> 00:21:20.909
And he writes “What would your advice be
to aspiring filmmakers?”

00:21:20.909 --> 00:21:25.030
Let’s suppose someone is 14 again but this
time instead of mini-DV they have 4k, this

00:21:25.030 --> 00:21:29.080
amazing camera and they don’t know what
to do with it?

00:21:29.080 --> 00:21:31.269
Bing: Do it for the love.

00:21:31.269 --> 00:21:36.900
When I was growing up in Rockford, being a
filmmaker was just such a realistic thing.

00:21:36.900 --> 00:21:38.309
I was just doing it for the love.

00:21:38.309 --> 00:21:40.100
I was emulating things.

00:21:40.100 --> 00:21:49.299
It felt good to be creative but in terms of
making a film you know these things take years.

00:21:49.299 --> 00:21:54.649
So don’t pick something that you’re not
truly passionate about, pick something that

00:21:54.649 --> 00:21:59.210
you care about more than just making a film
about.

00:21:59.210 --> 00:22:03.590
And I think everyone because of their personal
experience, because of where they come from,

00:22:03.590 --> 00:22:07.479
because they way they think, everyone has
at least one story within themselves that

00:22:07.479 --> 00:22:11.960
only they can tell and nobody else can tell
and I think people should honor and respect

00:22:11.960 --> 00:22:13.909
that for themselves.

00:22:13.909 --> 00:22:17.700
And I don’t just mean making a personal
film about themselves.

00:22:17.700 --> 00:22:25.630
It’s just that we really need to sort of
have understanding across many different aisles

00:22:25.630 --> 00:22:31.999
and lines that seem a little bit more too
highlighted these days lines of division.

00:22:31.999 --> 00:22:37.820
Film Courage: What did you use to film the
movie, from the mini-DV to…I mean there

00:22:37.820 --> 00:22:41.159
were definitely shots in the film that were
not mini-DV.

00:22:41.159 --> 00:22:44.779
Bing: I consider all that stuff archival.

00:22:44.779 --> 00:22:50.159
So I filmed it with three different DSLRs
and (Canon) C300.

00:22:50.159 --> 00:22:54.899
Film Courage: Did you outline the documentary
or did you have any kind of story structure

00:22:54.899 --> 00:22:56.169
to it?

00:22:56.169 --> 00:23:03.970
Bing: Yes, it started out in probably…I’ve
done like 40 grant applications in those years

00:23:03.970 --> 00:23:09.679
and got rejected for 39 of them…38 of them.

00:23:09.679 --> 00:23:17.169
But grant applications ask for either a treatment
or a synopsis or something right.

00:23:17.169 --> 00:23:24.039
And then as I got deeper into editing I put
scenes on Post-it notes (I put them on the

00:23:24.039 --> 00:23:25.039
wall).

00:23:25.039 --> 00:23:28.850
I kept a very detailed log while I was shooting
and that comes from me working in the camera

00:23:28.850 --> 00:23:33.529
department for money to pay rent.

00:23:33.529 --> 00:23:38.779
And then in the final year I moved to an online
whiteboard that Josh Altman turned me onto

00:23:38.779 --> 00:23:40.220
called RealTimeBoard.

00:23:40.220 --> 00:23:47.109
It’s a lot easier than writing on Post-it
notes and sticky notes get unsticky and they

00:23:47.109 --> 00:23:48.109
fall.

00:23:48.109 --> 00:23:52.489
It’s all digital and it’s online and you
can share between different users and you

00:23:52.489 --> 00:23:56.789
can instantly duplicate a whole thing and
then rearrange stuff.

00:23:56.789 --> 00:24:04.059
So there are several different versions of
both written treatments and scene outlines.

00:24:04.059 --> 00:24:07.649
Film Courage: In the end, what do you want
people to gain from the film?

00:24:07.649 --> 00:24:11.899
I mean there are so many themes to this films
and so many takeaways.

00:24:11.899 --> 00:24:18.989
It’s about family, fatherhood, breaking
out of a little town or deciding to stay.

00:24:18.989 --> 00:24:23.019
What do you want people to gain from MINDING
THE GAP?

00:24:23.019 --> 00:24:24.859
Bing: Life moves really fast.

00:24:24.859 --> 00:24:29.409
We go into our 20’s and we get a job, a
career, family (maybe).

00:24:29.409 --> 00:24:35.330
Then we turn 30 and all of the sudden we get
old.

00:24:35.330 --> 00:24:41.039
But then we’ve often experienced adolescence
as a time where our emotions are not taken

00:24:41.039 --> 00:24:46.419
seriously.They are brushed off as like just
grow up or oh, you’re being so emotional.

00:24:46.419 --> 00:24:53.669
And there are all these epithets that sort
of unconsciously tell us like oh no, we need

00:24:53.669 --> 00:24:55.630
to grow up, we need to grow up.

00:24:55.630 --> 00:25:01.889
But in fact that time of adolescence it’s
such a soft time, such a malleable time and

00:25:01.889 --> 00:25:05.509
it just informs who we become so much.

00:25:05.509 --> 00:25:10.710
Even in the little things like getting our
first job or taking about our father for the

00:25:10.710 --> 00:25:14.600
first time with a trusted friend, that stuff
really matters.

00:25:14.600 --> 00:25:18.990
And I think we should give it the respect
and the analysis that it deserves.

00:25:18.990 --> 00:25:21.769
I think it will help us understand ourselves
as adults.

00:25:21.769 --> 00:25:31.200
Also, we were talking about this off camera
but there are millions of Zack and Keire’s

00:25:31.200 --> 00:25:38.779
out there and not all of them happen to skateboard.

00:25:38.779 --> 00:25:42.249
These are issues that youth face across the
board.

00:25:42.249 --> 00:25:49.649
Film Courage: Yeah and I think what is interesting
too is it affects you even beyond thirty and

00:25:49.649 --> 00:25:55.759
that’s something that people don’t realize,
whether you are a parent or not.

00:25:55.759 --> 00:26:01.629
Bing: You can brush it off, you can sweep
it underneath the rug but I mean those seeds

00:26:01.629 --> 00:26:03.319
that are planted are going to grow.

